Run this in a ChucK runtime:
```chuck
SinOsc tone => dac;
0.05 => tone.gain;
220 => tone.freq;
250::ms => now;
```
The connection builds the audio graph. Advancing now lets synthesis run for the specified duration; simply constructing an oscillator does not keep a shred alive.

References:
https://chuck.stanford.edu/doc/reference/ugens-basic.html
https://chuck.stanford.edu/doc/language/time.html

Place a mono kick.wav beside the script, then try:
```chuck
SndBuf hit => dac;
me.dir() + "kick.wav" => hit.read;
0.15 => hit.gain;
for (0 => int i; i < 4; i++) {
    0 => hit.pos;
    500::ms => now;
}
```
Resetting pos retriggers playback. Confirm the file loaded. Reusing one player retriggers or cuts off its earlier hit; use separate voices for overlaps.

References:
https://chuck.stanford.edu/doc/reference/ugens-basic.html

For quarter-note BPM, use 60/BPM seconds per beat:
```chuck
110.0 => float bpm;
(60.0 / bpm)::second => dur beat;
4::beat => now;
```
This waits four beats in logical audio time. Keep the BPM definition explicit: a dotted-quarter pulse and a quarter-note pulse are different assumptions.

References:
https://chuck.stanford.edu/doc/language/time.html

With the SuperCollider server booted, try this original one-shot patch at low output level:
```supercollider
{
    var amp = EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.002, 0.3), doneAction: 2);
    var pitch = 48 + (100 * EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.001, 0.035)));
    (SinOsc.ar(pitch) * amp * 0.15) ! 2
}.play;
```
The pitch envelope creates the downward sweep; the amplitude envelope frees the synth when finished. Change decay and pitch sweep separately. This snippet has not been auditioned here.

References:
https://docs.supercollider.online/Classes/SinOsc.html
https://docs.supercollider.online/Classes/EnvGen.html

Inside a user-triggered handler, resume an existing AudioContext named ctx, then create an oscillator and gain:
```javascript
await ctx.resume();
const t = ctx.currentTime + 0.02;
const osc = ctx.createOscillator();
const amp = ctx.createGain();
osc.connect(amp).connect(ctx.destination);
osc.frequency.setValueAtTime(145, t);
osc.frequency.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(48, t + 0.08);
amp.gain.setValueAtTime(0, t);
amp.gain.linearRampToValueAtTime(0.15, t + 0.003);
amp.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.0001, t + 0.3);
osc.start(t);
osc.stop(t + 0.32);
```
Exponential ramps need positive endpoints. Recreate the oscillator for each hit.

References:
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/OscillatorNode
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AudioParam/exponentialRampToValueAtTime

In a p5.js sketch:
```javascript
function setup() { createCanvas(480, 320); }
function draw() {
  background(14, 20, 17);
  fill(196, 245, 134);
  circle(width / 2, height / 2, 90 + 20 * sin(millis() / 600));
}
```
This animation uses time, not microphone data. Treat it as a visual control test before connecting an audio feature. Create the main canvas once in setup.

References:
https://p5js.org/reference/p5/createCanvas/
